WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.750
The DeLorean DMC-12 drove its way into movie
history in "Back to the Future," but there's

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:07.336
a lot about this iconic car that you may not
know.

00:00:07.336 --> 00:00:11.470
From its cramped interior to its souped-up
engine, this is the history of everyone's

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:13.580
favorite two-door time machine.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:18.320
The true story behind the DeLorean DMC-12
is a wild one, and the man behind the iconic

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.820
car is a huge part of it.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:23.860
John DeLorean was the youngest division head
in General Motors' history, responsible for

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:27.710
the GTO and the Firebird when he was head
engineer at Pontiac.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:32.930
After the success of the GTO, DeLorean's personality
and lifestyle changed — and tensions between

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:35.273
him and other GM executives grew.

00:00:35.273 --> 00:00:41.180
After leaving GM, the maverick engineer founded
the DeLorean Motor Company in 1975, wanting

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:46.407
to disrupt the automotive industry with a
sports car that was safe, sustainable, and fuel-efficient.

00:00:46.407 --> 00:00:51.150
Despite its futuristic look and early interest,
though, the DMC-12 wasn't everything DeLorean

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:55.200
had promised — and the new motor company
experienced funding issues before the factory

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.950
in Northern Ireland began production.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:01.800
Giorgetto Giugiaro, the lauded automotive
designer, based the DMC-12 on his design for

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.446
the one-off concept car called the Porsche
Tapiro.

00:01:04.446 --> 00:01:09.450
Giugiaro also designed the Lotus Esprit, which
became James Bond's underwater car in 1977's

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:14.590
"The Spy Who Loved Me," suggesting that the
DeLorean was destined for the silver screen.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:20.400
In October of 1982, John DeLorean was arrested
for drug trafficking after meeting with agents

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.209
in an undercover sting operation.

00:01:22.209 --> 00:01:27.140
DeLorean was charged with conspiracy to distribute
55 pounds of cocaine, in what most believed

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:31.020
was a desperate attempt to raise funds to
save his fledgling motor company.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.870
Weeks after DeLorean's arrest, the DeLorean
Motor Company declared bankruptcy.

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:39.500
In 1984, DeLorean was acquitted by a jury
when they ruled that Hoffman and the FBI had

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.720
entrapped the automotive innovator — but
the damage had been done to both his company

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.350
and his public standing.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.860
DeLorean's financial and legal troubles continued
after DMC went belly-up.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:53.400
DeLorean was accused of misappropriating funds
and was sued by investors.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.773
"I have no hostility, no, no desire for revenge,
I'm not mad at anybody.

00:01:58.773 --> 00:02:00.490
I just want to get on with my life."

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:05.520
The British government also investigated DeLorean,
who faced trials for fraud and embezzlement.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.340
Civil suits settled in the decades following
the dissolution of his company drove DeLorean

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:13.790
to bankruptcy, and he was forced to sell his
New Jersey estate to Donald Trump in 2000.

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:17.465
John DeLorean died in 2005 after suffering
a stroke.

00:02:17.465 --> 00:02:19.209
He was 80 years old.

00:02:19.209 --> 00:02:23.190
The time machine for the "Back to the Future"
trilogy wasn't always intended to be the iconic

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:24.190
DeLorean DMC-12.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:28.490
In an early iteration of the script, the time
machine was a refrigerator that Doc Brown

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:30.540
carted around in the back of a pickup.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.260
Director and co-writer Robert Zemeckis told
the Santa Barbara Film Festival,

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.280
"We actually had thought of putting a time
machine in a refrigerator at one point.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.596
But [...] we worried that kids would start
locking themselves in refrigerators."

00:02:41.596 --> 00:02:45.371
Not only was the time machine originally much
colder, but an early version of the "Back

00:02:45.371 --> 00:02:50.140
to the Future" script almost nuked the fridge
25 years before everyone's favorite archeologist

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:53.233
did so in "Indiana Jones and the Kingdom of
the Crystal Skull."

00:02:53.233 --> 00:02:57.150
In an early version of the script, the fridge
is taken to a nuclear test site, where it

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.634
harnesses the power of the nuclear blast to
send Marty McFly back to 1985.

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:05.650
After being told that the production needed
to cut the budget by $1 million, Zemeckis

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:10.100
and writer-producer Bob Gale eventually realized
that a lightning strike supplying 1.21 gigawatts

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:13.580
to the flux capacitor was a much more elegant
solution.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:18.069
Thus, "Back to the Future" was saved from
the ridicule "Indiana Jones" experienced after

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:21.950
the fourth film in his franchise depicted
a lead-lined fridge being used to survive

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:23.543
an atomic blast.

00:03:23.543 --> 00:03:27.300
After Robert Zemeckis and Bob Gale decided
that Doc Brown's time travel device should

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.670
be mobile, Zemickis had an epiphany.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.290
Gale told Box Office Mojo,

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:34.689
"Zemeckis came up with this idea that it would
be cool to use a DeLorean because it could

00:03:34.689 --> 00:03:36.565
be mistaken for a flying saucer."

00:03:36.565 --> 00:03:40.750
"The way I see it, if you're gonna build a
time machine into a car, why not do it with

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:41.750
some style?"

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:45.239
The futuristic look of the DeLorean was an
important reason for the decision.

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:47.749
Zemeckis told the Hollywood Foreign Press
Association,

00:03:47.749 --> 00:03:51.769
"We chose the DeLorean basically because it
was the only car that existed that had gull-wing

00:03:51.769 --> 00:03:55.470
doors and would look kind of like a spaceship
to someone in the '50s."

00:03:55.470 --> 00:04:00.610
When Marty crashes into a barn on Mr. Peabody's
farm after arriving in 1955, Peabody's son

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:04.830
is quick to make the connection between the
DMC-12 and a spaceship, showing his family

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:08.502
the cover of the comic book he was reading
about aliens from outer space.

00:04:08.502 --> 00:04:12.761
After Robert Zemeckis and Bob Gale decided
that their time machine should be a car, automotive

00:04:12.761 --> 00:04:14.454
giants courted the studio.

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:20.183
Ford reportedly offered the studio $75,000
if they used a Mustang in "Back to the Future."

00:04:20.183 --> 00:04:24.800
This wasn't an unreasonable request, considering
how the liberal use of product placement helped

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.340
display the time periods Marty and Doc visit
during the three films.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:30.866
Still, Gale was quick to shoot the offer down
by saying,

00:04:30.866 --> 00:04:33.410
"Doc Brown doesn't drive a f---ing Mustang!"

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:37.259
The studio handled much of the movie's product
placement without input from Zemeckis and

00:04:37.259 --> 00:04:41.230
Gale, but the co-creators of "Back to the
Future" had their hearts set on the DeLorean.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:46.419
They made a licensing deal with John DeLorean
in 1989, giving Universal Studios merchandising

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:50.270
and commercial use of the car's design for
5% of the revenue.

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:55.345
Universal Pictures used six DMC-12s and one
fiberglass replica for the "Back to the Future" trilogy.

00:04:55.345 --> 00:05:00.120
Robert Zemeckis and Bob Gale gave their designers
little guidance, but insisted that the machine

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:04.250
look homemade, as the eccentric Doc Brown
had constructed it in the privacy of his garage.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:09.100
Artist Ron Cobb, illustrator Andy Probert
and production designer Larry Paull worked

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.966
on renderings for the time machine.

00:05:10.966 --> 00:05:15.540
Mike Scheffe, the vehicle construction coordinator,
along with special effects supervisor Kevin

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:20.580
Pike and his team, gave multiple DeLoreans
a makeover for the "Back to the Future" trilogy.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:24.710
Keeping the DMC-12s in tip-top shape during
production was a constant struggle, as the

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:27.788
cars were quite fragile and broke down often
during filming.

00:05:27.788 --> 00:05:32.310
There were three cars modified for the first
film: car "A" was the big star, while car

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:35.449
"B" did stunt work and car "C" was used for
interior shots.

00:05:35.449 --> 00:05:38.490
For the second and third films, more DeLoreans
joined the fleet.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:42.419
The fiberglass replica was used for flying
scenes, and the "B car" was modified to fit

00:05:42.419 --> 00:05:44.620
on the railroad tracks for the third film.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:48.919
Finally, "desert car #1" and "desert car #2"
were used for the last movie in the trilogy,

00:05:48.919 --> 00:05:52.580
when Marty travels to the Old West to save
Doc Brown.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.860
During his time running his company, John
DeLorean had plans to make a turbocharged

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:57.409
version of the DMC-12.

00:05:57.409 --> 00:06:02.103
He even had a contract with Legend Industries,
a U.S.-based company that would handle the conversion.

00:06:02.103 --> 00:06:06.910
Sadly, his plans for a turbocharged DMC-12
were scrapped before the DeLorean Motor Company

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:11.520
went bankrupt, as Legend Industries itself
went bankrupt while waiting for DMC to fix

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:13.667
their production and funding issues.

00:06:13.667 --> 00:06:18.949
The DMC-12 had 130 horsepower and accelerated
from zero to 60 miles per hour in just over

00:06:18.949 --> 00:06:22.639
10 seconds, which wasn't as good as its contemporary
competition.

00:06:22.639 --> 00:06:27.830
In 1981, one of these competitors was the
Porsche 911, another rear-engine sports car

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:29.389
that was lighter and faster.

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:35.000
Therefore, Universal Studios decided to replace
the DMC-12's original V6 engine with one from

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.118
a Porsche instead.

00:06:36.118 --> 00:06:41.410
This alteration essentially created a supercharged
version of the DMC-12, similar to what DeLorean

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:44.810
planned on offering in response to complaints
about the car's speed and power.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:47.319
"Does it run, like, on regular unleaded gasoline?"

00:06:47.319 --> 00:06:50.361
"Unfortunately no, it requires something with
a little more kick.

00:06:50.361 --> 00:06:51.361
Plutonium!"

00:06:51.361 --> 00:06:57.440
The original speedometer of the DMC-12 only
reached 85 miles per hour, as a 1979 mandate

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:01.660
signed into law by President Jimmy Carter
capped them at that number to deter speeding.

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:05.820
The effects department of "Back to the Future"
had to swap out the DeLorean's original speedometer

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:11.300
with one that reached 95mph, and they also
added a digital one to the dashboard for accuracy

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:12.706
and dramatic effect.

00:07:12.706 --> 00:07:16.889
While this might seem like a minor detail,
Doc Brown's DeLorean must hit 88 miles an

00:07:16.889 --> 00:07:21.316
hour to enter the past or future, making the
enhancement absolutely necessary.

00:07:21.316 --> 00:07:25.136
But why was 88 miles per hour the number Zemickis
and Gale decided on?

00:07:25.136 --> 00:07:29.040
There are some scientific theories about rates
of speed and wormholes in the "Back to the

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:31.704
Future" universe, but the truth is apparently
much more simple.

00:07:31.704 --> 00:07:33.635
Gale reportedly told ComicBook,

00:07:33.635 --> 00:07:37.539
"First of all we wanted it to be a speed that
somebody wouldn't accidentally drive at.

00:07:37.539 --> 00:07:39.574
The other thing is, it's easy to remember."

00:07:39.574 --> 00:07:45.300
"When this baby hits 88 miles per hour…
you're gonna see some serious s---.”

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:49.509
Although "Back to the Future" secured the
DeLorean DMC-12 a place in cinematic history,

00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:52.509
the filmmakers admitted it wasn't always a
cooperative star.

00:07:52.509 --> 00:07:53.864
Bob Gale told Esquire,

00:07:53.864 --> 00:07:57.110
"As cool as it looks on film, it's by no means
a performance car.

00:07:57.110 --> 00:08:01.360
It broke down a lot, and little things on
the car would break during a scene, and we'd

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:03.440
have to wait for the FX guys to repair it."

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.099
The gull-wing doors on the DMC-12 ruined multiple
takes during one exceptionally chilly night

00:08:08.099 --> 00:08:09.970
while filming the lightning strike scene.

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:14.379
The doors of the DMC-12 are supposed to remain
up thanks to gas-filled cylinders, but the

00:08:14.379 --> 00:08:16.807
doors wouldn't stay open in the frigid conditions.

00:08:16.807 --> 00:08:20.320
The crew resorted to warming the cylinders
with hair dryers between takes to keep the

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:21.979
doors open while filming.

00:08:21.979 --> 00:08:26.040
Although Michael J. Fox and Christopher Lloyd
became close friends over the years, Fox never

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.849
expressed kind words about his automotive
co-star.

00:08:28.849 --> 00:08:30.000
Fox joked to Nerdist,

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.757
"I wish I had a video, a gag reel of how many
times I was hit by that freakin' door, the

00:08:34.757 --> 00:08:35.512
DeLorean door.

00:08:35.512 --> 00:08:37.551
Take after take, it would go, WHACK!"

00:08:37.551 --> 00:08:42.010
Despite not enjoying working with the DMC-12,
Fox must have recognized they made a dynamic

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:43.473
duo on-screen.

00:08:43.473 --> 00:08:48.210
One of the DMC-12s used in "Back to the Future,"
known as the "C" car, was cut apart in order

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:50.459
to film interior shots of the time machine.

00:08:50.459 --> 00:08:54.630
The cabin of a DeLorean, especially with all
the stylistic modifications, was simply too

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:59.029
cramped for a 35-millimeter Panaflex camera
and an actor to fit inside.

00:08:59.029 --> 00:09:03.230
That's why the scenes filmed in the "C" car
were shot on the backlot of Universal Studios,

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:06.589
and the background outside the cabin of the
DMC-12 was projected.

00:09:06.589 --> 00:09:10.080
The "C" car ended up being used for interior
shots in all three films.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:14.600
Michael J. Fox reportedly hated working with
the DeLorean while filming the sci-fi trilogy,

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.003
and the cramped interior was a source of his
hatred.

00:09:17.003 --> 00:09:18.162
He told Nerdist,

00:09:18.162 --> 00:09:19.680
"It was the worst thing to drive.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:24.080
It had two gears, and I slammed the gears,
and my hand would smash into the flux capacitor.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:27.840
My hand would be bleeding, my head would be
bleeding [...] and I had to clean it off to

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:29.505
get to the 'Family Ties' set."

00:09:29.505 --> 00:09:31.355
"Let's land on him, we'll cripple his car."

00:09:31.355 --> 00:09:33.220
"Marty, he's in a '46 Ford, we're in a DeLorean.

00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:35.220
He'd rip through us like we were tin foil."

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:39.470
The "hero car," or car "A" — which was featured
in most scenes and beauty shots from the "Back

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:43.780
to the Future" trilogy — was used by Universal
Pictures and Universal Studios for promotional

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.209
purposes for years.

00:09:45.209 --> 00:09:49.250
The "Back to the Future" theme park ride was
filmed with the "A" car, but after years of

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:52.610
use, it was left to the elements in the park
and began to deteriorate.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:55.910
Fans took pieces of the car home, and it was
in sorry shape.

00:09:55.910 --> 00:10:00.320
The "hero car" was given a museum-quality
restoration by Terry Matalas and Joe Walser,

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:04.220
who had already made a couple of replicas
before convincing Universal to allow a team

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:05.640
to take on the restoration.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:10.379
The 20-person team spent over a year restoring
the DeLorean to its pristine film-star condition

00:10:10.379 --> 00:10:14.920
— and after the restoration was completed
in 2013, the "hero car" was displayed at the

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:16.460
Universal Experience.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:21.340
In 2016, the hero car was loaned to Petersen
Automotive Museum in Los Angeles, and it's

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:23.010
been on display there ever since.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:28.079
In 2021, the "hero car" became the 29th car
added to the National Historic Vehicle Register

00:10:28.079 --> 00:10:30.130
because of its cultural significance.

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:34.230
The fiberglass replica that was used for the
flying scenes in the sequel was put on display

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:37.329
at Universal Studios, but was destroyed years
later.

00:10:37.329 --> 00:10:41.070
The "B" car, nicknamed the "wreckage car,"
did all the stunts and was destroyed by a

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:44.480
train after it was retrofitted with train
wheels for the third film.

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:48.170
Parts of the "B" car were used in replicas,
and some panels were hung from the ceiling

00:10:48.170 --> 00:10:49.820
of Planet Hollywood in Hawaii.

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:55.170
The car later became part of a private collection
owned by father-son duo Bill and Patrick Shea.

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:59.070
Many parts from the "C" car, which had already
been cut apart, were used to build a replica

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:01.060
for Universal Studios in Japan.

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:05.187
A private company ultimately purchased this
replica, and they displayed it at their entrance.

00:11:05.187 --> 00:11:09.310
The "Oxnard car," which was used during filming
of "Back to the Future Part III" in Oxnard,

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:13.410
California, was displayed at Universal Studios
Florida before being put in storage, where

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:14.410
it still languishes.

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:18.400
Two "desert" cars were also used while filming
the third movie in Monument Valley.

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:22.280
One was restored for a private collection,
and the other was initially used for parts.

00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:26.540
What remained was later combined with parts
from the "C" car to make the replica for display in Japan.

